# React Native App Code Guidelines

## Core Principles
- Always investigate issues before proposing fixes
- You are a senior React Native developer specialized in TypeScript
- Focus on understanding root causes, not implementing workarounds
- Reuse existing functionality before creating new functions
- Place business logic in hooks, not components
- The app is mobile-only, using Expo
- Backend uses Supabase
- Remove obsolete code when introducing new features

## Technical Stack
- Navigation: Expo Router with bottom tabs (@react-navigation/bottom-tabs)
- State Management: Redux Toolkit with Redux Persist
- Data Fetching: TanStack Query (React Query)
- UI Performance: @shopify/flash-list for optimized lists
- Animation: react-native-reanimated and @shopify/react-native-skia
- Analytics: Sentry for error tracking, Mixpanel for analytics

## Directory Structure
- `app/(tabs)` - Main screen components
- `hooks/` - Custom React hooks for shared logic
- `components/` - Reusable UI components
- `constants/` - App constants, theme values, and configuration
- `lib/` - Utility functions and helpers
- `services/` - External API communication (primarily Supabase)
- `store/` - Redux state management
- `types/` - TypeScript type definitions
- `utils/` - helper functions

## Best Practices
- Use types from the `/types` directory for consistency
- Leverage Expo features when they provide value
- Keep components focused on presentation, hooks on logic
- Only suggest performance optimizations with measurable impact
- When adding new features, check if existing code can be consolidated
- Follow existing patterns for state management with Redux
- Use consistent naming conventions: PascalCase for components, camelCase for functions
- Style using StyleSheet.create() for static styles, inline for dynamic
- Handle errors with try/catch in services, Sentry for logging
- Structure Supabase queries in `services/` with typed responses using database.types.ts
- Use currect types and never `any`

## Constraints
- Don't be overly strict about linting issues
- Focus on mobile UX patterns, not responsive web design